  "learningBlocks": [
    {
      "id": "l18-b1",
      "type": "concept",
      "title": "Not every 'he/this/that' does the same job",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Latin has several pronoun families for pointing, identifying and intensifying. hic points to 'this'; ille and iste point to 'that'; ipse intensifies identity; idem means 'the same'. is/ea/id remains the lighter anaphoric third-person family.",
        "fr": "Le latin possède plusieurs familles pour montrer, identifier et renforcer. hic désigne « ceci » ; ille et iste « cela » ; ipse renforce l'identité ; idem signifie « le même ». is/ea/id reste la famille anaphorique plus légère."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l18-b2",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "Hoc est enim Corpus meum",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-consecration-hoc-l18",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Hoc is neuter because it identifies Corpus, a neuter noun. The demonstrative does not merely translate 'this'; its form reveals gender, number and case.",
        "fr": "Hoc est neutre parce qu'il identifie Corpus, nom neutre. Le démonstratif ne se contente pas de traduire « ceci » : sa forme révèle genre, nombre et cas."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-consecration-hoc-l18",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l18-b3",
      "type": "authentic-example",
      "title": "In ipso vita erat",
      "sourceId": "mr1962-last-gospel-ipso-l18",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "ipso is ablative after in. ipse adds intensive identity: 'in Him / in that very one'. Case government and pronoun nuance work together.",
        "fr": "ipso est à l'ablatif après in. ipse ajoute une valeur intensive d'identité : « en lui / en celui-là même ». Régime de cas et nuance pronominale fonctionnent ensemble."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "mr1962-last-gospel-ipso-l18",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l18-b4",
      "type": "contrast",
      "title": "ille and iste",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "Non erat ille lux uses ille to point back to a person already in discourse. Vere Filius Dei erat iste uses iste for direct identification. Context matters more than forcing one rigid English equivalent.",
        "fr": "Non erat ille lux emploie ille pour reprendre une personne déjà présente dans le discours. Vere Filius Dei erat iste emploie iste pour une identification directe. Le contexte compte plus qu'une traduction anglaise ou française unique."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l18-b5",
      "type": "morphology",
      "title": "unus and the pronominal-adjective pattern",
      "sourceId": "vulg-eph4-5-l18",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "unus, una, unum agrees like an adjective, but its genitive/dative singular forms follow a pronominal pattern. solus, totus and alius belong to the same useful morphological family.",
        "fr": "unus, una, unum s'accorde comme un adjectif, mais son génitif et son datif singuliers suivent un modèle pronominal. solus, totus et alius appartiennent à la même famille morphologique utile."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "vulg-eph4-5-l18",
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "l18-b6",
      "type": "pronoun",
      "title": "The spiral now requires family selection",
      "learnerCopy": {
        "en": "By Lesson 18 you must decide not only case, but pronoun family: personal, reflexive, anaphoric is/ea/id, relative qui/quae/quod, demonstrative hic/ille/iste, intensive ipse, or identity idem.",
        "fr": "À la leçon 18, il faut choisir non seulement le cas mais aussi la famille : personnel, réfléchi, anaphorique is/ea/id, relatif qui/quae/quod, démonstratif hic/ille/iste, intensif ipse ou identité idem."
      },
      "referenceIds": [
        "grammar-scanlon-1944",
        "grammar-nunn-1922",
        "grammar-petitmangin-1930",
        "curriculum-latin-course-40-v1"
      ]
    }
  ],
